Cell-Style Compute Model — High-Level Technical Design

Status: Draft 0.1
Purpose: Build a small x86-64 application that demonstrates the Cell-style way of organizing work and data—not a PS3 emulator or a performance model.

    “One general-purpose PPE coordinates work across a pool of specialized SPEs. The PPE dispatches tasks and manages control flow; each SPE is a focused worker, efficient at data-parallel jobs, with its own local memory and explicit data transfers.”

1. Scope and guiding principles

The first version runs on x86-64 only. It models one PPE and a configurable pool of SPEs; it does not reproduce Cell instructions, timings, cache behavior, or performance.

The design should make four ideas visible:

    Central coordination: the PPE divides work, schedules it, and collects results.
    Specialized workers: SPEs execute bounded tasks rather than managing the whole application.
    Explicit data movement: an SPE works on data staged into its own local workspace.
    Replaceable workers: the PPE communicates through a stable interface, so an SPE can be implemented in Python first and Java later.

The main architectural choice is to make SPEs separate worker processes from the beginning. They can all be written in Python initially, while a language-neutral process protocol makes replacing one or more with Java less disruptive than changing an in-process Python API.
2. Main components

PPE — coordinator and scheduler
Accepts a job, divides it into tasks, assigns tasks to SPEs, and handles results and errors. It owns orchestration and global job state; it does not directly perform all worker computation.

SPE pool — task workers
A configurable set of workers. Each worker accepts a task, stages its input into a bounded local workspace, performs its assigned operation, and returns a result or error. Worker implementations may differ in language.

Task and data model
A task describes what operation to perform and which data to use, without assuming a particular worker language. Inputs and outputs have identifiers, sizes, and formats. The model should support splitting a large job into independent chunks.

Transfer layer — the conceptual “DMA” path
Moves data between the PPE’s job memory and a worker’s local workspace. In the first version, transfers can simply be explicit copies. The important feature is that transfers are visible, measurable, and separate from computation.

Worker adapter — the replaceable interface
Starts workers, sends tasks and input data, receives results, and reports worker health. The PPE depends on this interface, not on Python-specific worker code.

Metrics and trace log
Records task assignment, transfer sizes, worker execution, queue wait, failures, and result collection. This lets the demo show the consequences of task size, data locality, and worker count.
3. Data flow
text

Job submitted     |     vPPE validates job and divides it into tasks     |     vPPE schedules tasks across available SPEs     |     vTransfer layer stages each task's inputinto that SPE's bounded local workspace     |     vSPE executes its assigned operation     |     vSPE returns result or error     |     vPPE collects results and assembles final output

A typical task lifecycle is:

    The PPE receives a job and breaks it into independent chunks.
    The scheduler assigns each chunk to an available SPE.
    The transfer layer copies the chunk’s input to that SPE’s local workspace.
    The SPE runs its operation on the staged input; it does not reach directly into arbitrary global job state.
    The SPE returns output data and a status.
    The PPE combines results, records metrics, and completes the job.

For the initial demo, use an array or image-processing workload with independent chunks. It makes partitioning, local staging, parallel workers, and result assembly easy to observe.
4. Worker interface

Keep the interface small and versioned. Conceptually, it needs to support:

    Start / ready: launch a worker and learn which operations it supports.
    Load data: transfer an input buffer or task data to the worker.
    Execute: submit a task using named operation and parameters.
    Return result: provide output data, status, and task identifier.
    Cancel / stop: stop work cleanly and shut down the worker.

Use a language-neutral, length-prefixed protocol over the worker process’s standard input/output for the first version. A simple JSON control message plus a binary payload is adequate for a prototype. Keep logs on standard error so they cannot corrupt protocol messages.

Initially, transfer payloads by copying them through the protocol. If measurement shows this is a bottleneck, add shared-memory buffers behind the same adapter. That keeps the initial design simpler while preserving an optimization path.
5. Python parallelism and worker execution

As of October 18, 2026, Python 3.15.0 is the newest stable major release. python.org For the PPE, use Python’s asynchronous I/O for worker lifecycle and coordination, and keep scheduling decisions explicit and inspectable.

For parallel execution, keep the choice behind the worker adapter rather than making the PPE depend on one Python concurrency mechanism. Python’s free-threaded build is supported but remains optional; test it as an execution option, since extensions must also be compatible with free-threading. 

 A process-based worker setup is a good initial default: it provides isolation, mirrors the eventual Java process boundary, and does not require the PPE to depend on free-threaded behavior. Benchmark free-threaded Python and Python’s isolated-interpreter options separately; use whichever fits the workload and dependencies.

Important distinction: free-threading is an implementation choice for running Python code concurrently. It is not the project’s architecture. The architecture is the PPE/SPE division, explicit data movement, and replaceable worker interface.
6. What the first version will model

    A single PPE with a task queue and scheduler.
    A configurable number of SPEs, each with a configurable local workspace limit.
    Explicit input and result transfers.
    Basic task splitting, worker assignment, error handling, and result assembly.
    Metrics for bytes transferred, queue time, execution time, and worker utilization.
    A Python worker implementation that can later be replaced by a Java worker using the same protocol.

The first version should not claim to reproduce Cell hardware performance or the details of its memory system. Transfer costs can initially be recorded as byte counts; configurable simulated delays can be added later to demonstrate how transfer volume and task size affect the workflow.
